  "summary": "The San Mateo County Civil Grand Jury released a report calling for stricter oversight of ALPR use by the county's 18 law enforcement agencies, finding deficient policy documents, inconsistent audit procedures, and improper in-state data sharing (including with private university security). The report requires responses from the sheriff and city councils, and references incidents where Menlo Park and Atherton allowed out-of-state access, and Redwood City facing pressure over its Flock contract.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Most operators did not document criteria, process, or controls governing in-state sharing in a manner sufficient to demonstrate compliance with law",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "all but one of the 18 law enforcement agencies shared data with private university security departments without establishing whether that entity qualified as a public agency",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "Police departments from Menlo Park and Atherton are among those that accidentally let out-of-state agencies tap into their Flock license plate reader systems, while Redwood City is under pressure to drop Flock as its vendor",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    }
  ],
